#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:9
msgid "Solving the heat equation in PyBaMM"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:11
msgid ""
"In this notebook we create and solve a model for unsteady heat diffusion in "
"1D, with a spatially dependent heat source term. The notebook is adapted "
"from example 4.1.2 on pg.16 of the online notes found `here <https://faculty."
"uca.edu/darrigo/Students/M4315/Fall%202005/sep-var.pdf>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:13
msgid "We consider the heat equation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:15
msgid ""
"T_{t} = kT_{xx} + Q(x), \\quad 0 < x < L, \\quad t > 0,\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:17
msgid "along with the boundary and initial conditions,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:19
msgid ""
"u(0, t)=0, \\quad u(L, t)=0, \\quad u(x, 0)=2x-x^2,\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:21
msgid "and heat source term"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:23
msgid ""
"Q(x)=1-|x-1|.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:25
msgid ""
"As in the example, we solve the problem on the domain :math:`0 < x < 2` (i."
"e. we take :math:`L=2`). We extended the example to include a thermal "
"diffusivity :math:`k`, which we take to be equal to 0.75."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:37
msgid "Building the model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:39
msgid ""
"As always, we start by importing PyBaMM, along with any other packages we "
"require."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:-1
msgid "[1]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"%pip install \"pybamm[plot,cite]\" -q    # install PyBaMM if it is not "
"installed\n"
"import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n"
"import numpy as np\n"
"\n"
"import pybamm"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:64
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"Note: you may need to restart the kernel to use updated packages.\n"
"</pre>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:70
msgid ""
"\\begin{sphinxVerbatim}[commandchars=\\\\\\{\\}]\n"
"Note: you may need to restart the kernel to use updated packages.\n"
"\\end{sphinxVerbatim}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:77
msgid "Note: you may need to restart the kernel to use updated packages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:88
msgid "We then load up an instance of the ``pybamm.BaseModel`` class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:-1
msgid "[2]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:-1
msgid "model = pybamm.BaseModel()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:109
msgid ""
"We now define the model variables and parameters. Note that we also need to "
"define the spatial variable :math:`x` here, so that we can write down the "
"spatially dependent source term :math:`Q(x)`. Since we are solving in 1D we "
"have decided to call the domain \"rod\", but we could name it anything we "
"like. Note that in PyBaMM variables and parameters can be given useful and "
"meaningful names, such as \"Temperature\", so that they can be easily "
"referred to later."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:-1
msgid "[3]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"x = pybamm.SpatialVariable(\"x\", domain=\"rod\", coord_sys=\"cartesian\")\n"
"T = pybamm.Variable(\"Temperature\", domain=\"rod\")\n"
"k = pybamm.Parameter(\"Thermal diffusivity\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:132
msgid ""
"Now that we have defined the variables, we can write down the model "
"equations and add them to the ``model.rhs`` dictionary. This dictionary "
"stores the right hand sides of any time-dependent differential equations "
"(ordinary or partial). The key is the variable inside the time derivative "
"(in this case :math:`T`). To define the heat source term we use a ``pybamm."
"Function`` class. The first argument of the class is the function, and the "
"second argument is the input."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:-1
msgid "[4]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"N = -k * pybamm.grad(T)  # Heat flux\n"
"Q = 1 - pybamm.Function(np.abs, x - 1)  # Source term\n"
"dTdt = -pybamm.div(N) + Q  # The right hand side of the PDE\n"
"model.rhs = {T: dTdt}  # Add to model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:156
msgid ""
"We now add the boundary conditions into the ``model.boundary_conditions`` "
"dictionary. The keys of the dictionary indicate which end of the boundary "
"the condition is applied to (in 1D this can be \"left\" or \"right\"), the "
"entry is then give as a tuple of the value and type. In this example we have "
"homogeneous Dirichlet boundary conditions at both ends."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:-1
msgid "[5]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"model.boundary_conditions = {\n"
"    T: {\n"
"        \"left\": (pybamm.Scalar(0), \"Dirichlet\"),\n"
"        \"right\": (pybamm.Scalar(0), \"Dirichlet\"),\n"
"    }\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:182
msgid ""
"We also need to add the initial conditions to the ``model."
"initial_conditions`` dictionary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:-1
msgid "[6]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:-1
msgid "model.initial_conditions = {T: 2 * x - x**2}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:203
msgid ""
"Finally, we add any output variables to the ``model.variables`` dictionary. "
"These variables can be easily accessed after the model has been solved. You "
"can add any variables of interest to this dictionary. Here we have added the "
"temperature, heat flux and heat source."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:-1
msgid "[7]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"model.variables = {\"Temperature\": T, \"Heat flux\": N, \"Heat source\": Q}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:225
msgid "Using the model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:227
msgid ""
"Now that the model has been constructed we can go ahead and define our "
"geometry and parameter values. We start by defining the geometry for our "
"\"rod\" domain. We need to define the spatial direction(s) in which spatial "
"operators act (such as gradients). In this case it is simply :math:`x`. We "
"then set the minimum and maximum values :math:`x` can take. In this example "
"we are solving the problem on the domain :math:`0<x<2`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:-1
msgid "[8]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"geometry = {\"rod\": {x: {\"min\": pybamm.Scalar(0), \"max\": pybamm."
"Scalar(2)}}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:248
msgid ""
"We also need to provide the values of any parameters using the ``pybamm."
"ParameterValues`` class. This class accepts a dictionary of parameter names "
"and values. Note that the name we provide is the string name of the "
"parameters and not its symbol."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:-1
msgid "[9]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:-1
msgid "param = pybamm.ParameterValues({\"Thermal diffusivity\": 0.75})"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:269
msgid ""
"Now that we have defined the geometry and provided the parameters values, we "
"can process the model."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:-1
msgid "[10]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"param.process_model(model)\n"
"param.process_geometry(geometry)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:291
msgid ""
"Before we disctretise the spatial operators, we must choose and mesh and a "
"spatial method. Here we choose to use a uniformly spaced 1D mesh with 30 "
"points, and discretise the equations in space using the finite volume "
"method. The information about the mesh is stored in a ``pybamm.Mesh`` "
"object, wheres the spatial methods are stored in a dictionary which maps "
"domain names to a spatial method. This allows the user to discretise "
"different (sub)domains in a problem using different spatial methods. All of "
"this information goes into a ``pybamm.Discretisation`` object, which accepts "
"a mesh and a dictionary of spatial methods."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:-1
msgid "[11]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"submesh_types = {\"rod\": pybamm.Uniform1DSubMesh}\n"
"var_pts = {x: 30}\n"
"mesh = pybamm.Mesh(geometry, submesh_types, var_pts)\n"
"spatial_methods = {\"rod\": pybamm.FiniteVolume()}\n"
"disc = pybamm.Discretisation(mesh, spatial_methods)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:317
msgid ""
"The model is then processed using the discretisation, turning the spaital "
"operators into matrix-vector multiplications."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:-1
msgid "[12]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:-1
msgid "disc.process_model(model)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:339
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"&lt;pybamm.models.base_model.BaseModel at 0x7f39430e6ad0&gt;\n"
"</pre>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:345
msgid ""
"\\begin{sphinxVerbatim}[commandchars=\\\\\\{\\}]\n"
"<pybamm.models.base\\_model.BaseModel at 0x7f39430e6ad0>\n"
"\\end{sphinxVerbatim}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:352
msgid "<pybamm.models.base_model.BaseModel at 0x7f39430e6ad0>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:363
msgid ""
"Now that the model has been discretised we are ready to solve. We must first "
"choose a solver to use. For this model we choose the Scipy ODE solver, but "
"other solvers are available in PyBaMM (see `here <https://docs.pybamm.org/en/"
"latest/source/api/solvers/index.html>`__). To solve the model, we use the "
"method ``solver.solve`` which takes in a model and an array of times at "
"which we would like the solution to be returned. Ths solution is then stored "
"in the ``solution`` object. The times and states can be accessed with "
"``solver.t`` and ``solver.y``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:-1
msgid "[13]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"solver = pybamm.ScipySolver()\n"
"t = np.linspace(0, 1, 100)\n"
"solution = solver.solve(model, t)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:387
msgid ""
"After solving, we can process variables using the class ``pybamm."
"ProcessedVariable``. This returns a callable object which can be evaluated "
"at any time and position by means of interpolating the solution. Processed "
"variables provide a convinient way of comparing the solution to solutions "
"from different models, or to exact solutions. Since all of the variables are "
"names with informative strings, the user doesn't need to keep track of where "
"they are stored in the state vector ``solution.y``. This is particularly "
"useful in complex models with lots of variables, and is automatically "
"handled by the solution dictionary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:-1
msgid "[14]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:-1
msgid "T_out = solution[\"Temperature\"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/unsteady-heat-equation.ipynb:410
